\id PRO - Brenton English Septuagint 
\h Proverbs 
\toc1 Proverbs 
\toc2 Proverbs 
\toc3 Prov. 
\mt1 PROVERBS 
\c 1 
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* Proverbs of Solomon son of David, who reigned in Israel; 
\q1
\v 2 to know wisdom and instruction,
\q2 and to perceive words of prudence; 
\q1
\v 3 to receive also subtle turns of speech, 
\q2 and to understand true righteousnss, 
\q2 and to guide judgment rightly; 
\q1
\v 4 that he might give shrewdness to the guileless, 
\q2 and to the young child discernment and understanding. 
\q1
\v 5 For by hearing these a wise man will be wiser, 
\q2 and the man of understanding will gain direction; 
\q1
\v 6 and will understand a parable, and a dark word; 
\q2 the sayings of the wise, and riddles. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom; 
\q2 and good understanding to all who do it: 
\q2 and piety toward God is the beginning of discernment; 
\q2 but the ungodly will despise wisdom and instruction. 
\q1
\v 8 Hear, \add my\add* son, the instruction of your father, 
\q2 and don't reject the rules of your mother. 
\q1
\v 9 For you will receive upon your head a crown of graces, 
\q2 and a chain of gold round your neck. 
\p
\q1
\v 10 \add My\add* son, don't let ungodly men lead you astray, 
\q2 nor consent if they urge you, saying, 
\q1
\v 11 Come with us, share in bloodshed, 
\q2 and let us hide the just man in the earth unjustly: 
\q1
\v 12 and let us swallow him alive, like Hades, 
\q2 and remove his memorial from the earth: 
\q1
\v 13 let us seize on his valuable property, 
\q2 and let us fill our houses with spoils: 
\q1
\v 14 but cast your lot in with us, 
\q2 and let us all have a common purse, 
\q2 and let us have one pouch: 
\q1
\v 15 don't go in the way with them, 
\q2 but turn aside your foot from their paths:
\q1
\v 16 For their feet run to evil,
\q2 and are swift to shed blood;
\q1
\v 17 for nets are not spread for birds without cause. 
\q1
\v 18 For those who take part in murder store up evils for themselves; 
\q2 and the overthrow of transgressors is \f + \fr 1:18 \fqa Gr. \ft evil. The sense is that it is disastrous for the transgressors, not that their overthrow is morally evil.\f*ruinous. 
\q1
\v 19 These are the ways of all who perform lawless deeds; 
\q2 for by ungodliness they destroy their own life. 
\p
\q1
\v 20 Wisdom is sung in thoroughfares, 
\q2 and in the broad places speaks boldly. 
\q1
\v 21 She makes proclamation on the heights of the walls, 
\q2 and sits by the gates of princes; 
\q2 and at the gates of the city boldly says, 
\q1
\v 22 As long as the innocent hold fast to righteousness, they will not be ashamed: 
\q2 but the foolish, being lovers of arrogance, 
\q2 having become ungodly, have hated knowledge, 
\q2 and have become subject to reproofs. 
\q1
\v 23 Behold, I will send forth to you the utterance of my breath, 
\q2 and I will instruct you in my word. 
\p
\q1
\v 24 Since I called, and you did not listen;
\q2 and I spoke at length, and you gave no heed; 
\q1
\v 25 but you set aside my counsels, 
\q2 and disregarded my reproofs; 
\q1
\v 26 therefore I also will laugh at your destruction; 
\q2 and I will exult when ruin comes upon you: 
\q1
\v 27 yes when terror suddenly comes upon you, 
\q2 and overthrow will arrive like a tempest; 
\q2 and when tribulation and siege come upon you, 
\q2 or when ruin comes upon you. 
\q1
\v 28 For it will be that when you call upon me, I will not listen to you: 
\q2 wicked men will seek me, but will not find \add me\add*. 
\q1
\v 29 For they hated wisdom, 
\q2 and did not choose the fear of the Lord: 
\q1
\v 30 neither would they pay attention to my counsels, 
\q2 but derided my reproofs. 
\q1
\v 31 Therefore will they eat the fruits of their own way, 
\q2 and will be filled with their own ungodliness. 
\q1
\v 32 For since they wronged the little ones, they will be killed; 
\q2 and scrutiny will ruin the ungodly. 
\q1
\v 33 But he who listens to me will dwell in hope, 
\q2 and will rest securely from all evil. 
\c 2 
\p
\q1
\v 1 \add My\add* son, if you will receive the utterance of my commandment, 
\q2 and hide it with you; 
\q1
\v 2 your ear will give heed to wisdom; 
\q2 you will also apply your heart to understanding, 
\q2 and you will apply it to instruct your son. 
\p
\q1
\v 3 For if you will call to wisdom, 
\q2 and utter your voice for understanding,
\q2 and seek discernment with a loud voice;
\q1
\v 4 and if you will seek it as silver, 
\q2 and search diligently for it as for treasures; 
\q1
\v 5 then will you understand the fear of the Lord, 
\q2 and find the knowledge of God. 
\p
\q1
\v 6 For the Lord gives wisdom; 
\q2 and from his presence \add come\add* knowledge and understanding, 
\q1
\v 7 and he treasures up salvation for those who act uprightly: 
\q2 he will protect their way; 
\q1
\v 8 that he may guard the ways of judgement: 
\q2 and he will preserve the way of those who reverence him. 
\q1
\v 9 Then will you understand righteousness, and judgment; 
\q2 and will make straight all good paths. 
\p
\q1
\v 10 For if wisdom will come into your mind, 
\q2 and discernment will seem pleasing to your soul, 
\q1
\v 11 good counsel will guard you, 
\q2 and holy thought will keep you; 
\q1
\v 12 to deliver you from the evil way, 
\q2 and from the man who speaks nothing trustworthy. 
\p
\q1
\v 13 Alas \add for those\add* who forsake right paths, 
\q2 to walk in ways of darkness; 
\q1
\v 14 who rejoice in evils, 
\q2 and delight in wicked perverseness; 
\q1
\v 15 whose paths are crooked, 
\q2 and their \f + \fr 2:15 \fqa Lit. \ft wheel tracks. Compare \xt Heb. 12. 13.\f*courses winding; 
\q1
\v 16 to remove you far from the straight way, 
\q2 and to estrange you from a righteous purpose. 
\q2 \add My\add* son, don't let evil counsel overtake you, 
\q1
\v 17 \add that of her\add* who has forsaken the instruction of her youth, 
\q2 and forgotten the covenant of God. 
\q1
\v 18 For she has fixed her house near death, 
\q2 and set her wheel-tracks near Hades with the earthborn ones. 
\q1
\v 19 None who go by her will return, 
\q2 neither will they take hold of right paths, 
\q2 for they are not overtaken by the years of life. 
\q1
\v 20 For had they gone in good paths, 
\q2 they would have found the paths of righteousness smooth. 
\q1
\v 21 \f + \fr 2:21 \ft Some Greek witnesses omit the first two lines of this verse.\f*For the good will be inhabitants of the earth,
\q2 and the innocent will be left behind in it;
\q1 For the upright will dwell in the earth, 
\q2 and the holy will be left behind in it. 
\q1
\v 22 The paths of the ungodly will perish out of the earth, 
\q2 and transgressors will be driven away from it. 
\c 3 
\p
\q1
\v 1 \add My\add* son, don't forget my laws; 
\q2 but let your heart keep my words: 
\q1
\v 2 for length of days, and years of life, 
\q2 and peace, will they add to you. 
\q1
\v 3 Don't let mercies and faithfulnesses forsake you; 
\q2 but bind them about your neck:
\q2 and you will find favour: 
\q1
\v 4 and take thought for honourable things
\q2 before the Lord and men. 
\p
\q1
\v 5 Trust in God with all your heart; 
\q2 and don't be puffed up in your own wisdom. 
\q1
\v 6 In all your ways acknowledge her, 
\q2 that she may rightly \f + \fr 3:6 \fqa Gr. \ft divide. See 2 Tim 2. 13.\f*direct your paths. 
\q1
\v 7 Don't be wise in your own conceit; 
\q2 but fear God, and depart from all evil. 
\q1
\v 8 Then will there be health to your body, 
\q2 and care for your bones. 
\p
\q1
\v 9 Honour the Lord with your just labours, 
\q2 and give him the first of your fruits of righteousness: 
\q1
\v 10 that your storehouses may be completely filled with grain, 
\q2 and that your presses may burst forth with wine. 
\p
\q1
\v 11 \x + \xo 3:11 \xt Heb. 12. 5,6.\x*\add My\add* son, don't despise the chastening of the Lord; 
\q2 nor faint when you are rebuked by him: 
\q1
\v 12 for whom the Lord loves, he chastens, 
\q2 and he scourges every son he receives. 
\p
\q1
\v 13 Blessed is the man who has found wisdom, 
\q2 and the mortal who has seen prudence. 
\q1
\v 14 For it is better to trade for her 
\q2 than treasures of gold and silver. 
\q1
\v 15 She is more valuable than precious stones: 
\q2 no evil thing will resist her: 
\q2 she is well known to all who approach her, 
\q2 and no precious thing is equal to her in value. 
\q1
\v 16 For length of days and years of life are in her right hand; 
\q2 and in her left hand are wealth and glory: 
\q1
\v 16a out of her mouth proceeds righteousness, 
\q2 and she carries law and mercy upon her tongue. 
\q1
\v 17 Her ways are good ways, 
\q2 and all her paths are peaceful. 
\q1
\v 18 She is a tree of life to all who lay hold upon her; 
\q2 and she is secure for all who lean on her, as on the Lord. 
\p
\q1
\v 19 God by wisdom founded the earth, 
\q2 and by prudence he prepared the heavens. 
\q1
\v 20 By understanding the depths were broken up, 
\q2 and the clouds poured out dew. 
\p
\q1
\v 21 \add My\add* son, don't let \add them\add* pass from \add you\add*, 
\q2 but keep my counsel and understanding: 
\q1
\v 22 that your soul may live, 
\q2 and that there may be grace round your neck; 
\q1
\v 22a and it will be health to your flesh, 
\q2 and care for your bones: 
\q1
\v 23 that you may go confidently in peace in all your ways, 
\q2 and that your foot may not stumble. 
\q1
\v 24 For if you \f + \fr 3:24 \fqa Gr. \ft sit down.\f*rest, you will be unafraid; 
\q2 and if you sleep, you will slumber sweetly. 
\q1
\v 25 You will not be afraid of alarm coming upon you, 
\q2 nor of approaching attacks of ungodly men. 
\q1
\v 26 For the Lord will be over all your ways, 
\q2 and will steady your foot so that you may not be shaken. 
\p
\q1
\v 27 Don't refrain from doing good to the needy, 
\q2 whenever your hand may have \add power\add* to help. 
\q1
\v 28 Don't say, Come back another time, tomorrow I will give; 
\q2 while you are able to do good: 
\q2 for you don't know what the next day will bring. 
\q1
\v 29 Don't devise evil against your friend, 
\q2 living near you and trusting in you. 
\p
\q1
\v 30 Don't be ready to quarrel with a man without a cause, 
\q2 lest he do you some harm. 
\p
\q1
\v 31 Do not incur the reproaches of evil men, 
\q2 nor envy their ways. 
\q1
\v 32 For every transgressor is unclean before the Lord; 
\q2 and among the righteous he does not take counsel. 
\q1
\v 33 The curse of God is in the houses of the ungodly; 
\q2 but the dwellings of the righteous are blessed. 
\q1
\v 34 The Lord resists the proud; 
\q2 but he gives grace to the humble. 
\q1
\v 35 The wise will inherit glory; 
\q2 but the ungodly have exalted \add their own\add* dishonour. 
\c 4 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Hear, you children, the instruction of a father, 
\q2 and pay attention to know insight. 
\q1
\v 2 For I give you a good gift; 
\q2 don't forsake my law. 
\q1
\v 3 For I also was a son obedient to \add my\add* father, 
\q2 and loved in the sight of \add my\add* mother: 
\q1
\v 4 who spoke and instructed me, 
\q2 Let our word be fixed in your heart, 
\q1
\v 5  keep commandments, don't forget them: 
\q2 and do not neglect the utterance of my mouth.
\q1
\v 6 Don't forsake it, and it will uphold to you: 
\q2 love it, and it will keep you. 
\q1
\v 8 \f + \fr 4:8 \fqa Lit. \ft dig a trench about her.\f*Secure it, and it will exalt you: 
\q2 honour it, that it may embrace you; 
\q1
\v 9 that it may give to your head a crown of graces, 
\q2 and may shield you with a crown of delight. 
\p
\q1
\v 10 Hear, \add my\add* son, and receive my words; 
\q2 and the years of your life will be increased, 
\q2 that the ways of your life may be many. 
\q1
\v 11 For I teach you the ways of wisdom; 
\q2 and I cause you to go in right \f + \fr 4:11 \fqa Gr. \ft wheel-tracks.\f*paths. 
\q1
\v 12 For when you go, your steps will not be constrained; 
\q2 and when you run, you will not grow weary. 
\q1
\v 13 Take hold of my instruction; 
\q2 don't let it go,—but keep it for yourself as your life. 
\p
\q1
\v 14 Don't go in the ways of the ungodly, 
\q2 nor envy the ways of transgressors. 
\q1
\v 15 In whatever place they pitch their camp, don't go there; 
\q2 but turn from them, and pass by. 
\q1
\v 16 For they cannot sleep, unless they have done evil: 
\q2 their sleep is taken away, and they don't rest. 
\q1
\v 17 For these feed on the bread of ungodliness, 
\q2 and are drunk with wine of transgression. 
\q1
\v 18 But the ways of the righteous shine like light; 
\q2 they go on and shine, until the day \f + \fr 4:18 \fqa Gr. \ft orders itself aright.\f*fully comes. 
\q1
\v 19 But the ways of the ungodly are dark; 
\q2 they don't know how they stumble. 
\p
\q1
\v 20 \add My\add* son, pay attention to my utterance; 
\q2 and apply your ear to my words: 
\q1
\v 21 that your fountains may not fail you; 
\q2 keep them in \add your\add* heart. 
\q1
\v 22 For they are life to those who find them, 
\q2 and \f + \fr 4:22 \fqa Or, \ft healing.\f*health to all \add their\add* flesh. 
\q1
\v 23 Keep your heart with the utmost care; 
\q2 for from these come the springs of life. 
\q1
\v 24 Remove from you a perverse mouth, 
\q2 and put far away from you unjust lips. 
\q1
\v 25 Let your eyes look straight, 
\q2 and let your eyelids look toward what is just.
\q1
\v 26 Make straight paths for your feet, 
\q2 and order your ways aright. 
\q1
\v 27 Don't turn aside to the right hand nor to the left, 
\q2 but turn your foot away from an evil way: 
\q1
\v 27a [for God knows the ways on the right hand, 
\q2 but those on the left are crooked:] 
\q1
\v 27b and he will make your paths straight, 
\q2 and will guide your ways in peace. 
\c 5 
\p
\q1
\v 1 \add My\add* son, pay attention to my wisdom, 
\q2 and apply your ear to my words; 
\q1
\v 2 that you may keep good insight, 
\q2 and I charge you with the discretion of my lips. 
\p 
\q1
\v 3 Give no heed to a worthless woman;
\q2 for honey drops from the lips of a prostitute, who for a season pleases your palate: 
\q1
\v 4 but afterwards you will find her \f + \fr 5:4 \fqa Gr. \ft a more bitter thing.\f*more bitter than gall, 
\q2 and sharper than a two-edged sword. 
\q1
\v 5 For the feet of folly lead those who deal with her down to Hades with death; 
\q2 and her steps are not established. 
\q1
\v 6 For she doesn't go upon the paths of life; 
\q2 but her ways are slippery, and not easily known. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 Now then, \add my\add* son, hear me, 
\q2 and don't make my words of no effect. 
\q1
\v 8 Remove your way far from her; 
\q2 don't draw near to the doors of her house: 
\q1
\v 9 lest you give away your life to others, 
\q2 and your livelihood to the merciless: 
\q1
\v 10 lest strangers be filled with your strength, 
\q2 and your labours come into the houses of strangers; 
\q1
\v 11 and you repent at last, 
\q2 when the flesh of your body is consumed, 
\q1
\v 12 and you will say, How have I hated instruction, 
\q2 and my heart turned aside from reproofs! 
\q1
\v 13 I didn't hear the voice of him who instructed me, and taught me, 
\q2 neither did I apply my ear. 
\q1
\v 14 I was almost in all evil among the congregation and assembly. 
\p
\q1
\v 15 Drink waters out of your own vessels, 
\q2 and out of your own wells of spring-water. 
\q1
\v 16 Don't let waters out of your fountain overflow, 
\q2 but let your waters go into your streets. 
\q1
\v 17 Let them be only your own, 
\q2 and let no stranger partake with you. 
\q1
\v 18 Let your fountain of water be your own; 
\q2 and rejoice with the wife of your youth. 
\q1
\v 19 Let the loving hind and the colt of your graces keep you company, 
\q2 and let your own wife lead you, and be with you at all times; 
\q2 for being carried along in her love, you will be greatly increased. 
\q1
\v 20 Don't spend much time with another woman, 
\q2 neither fold yourself in the arms of a woman not your own. 
\q1
\v 21 For the ways of a man are before the eyes of God, 
\q2 and he looks on all his paths. 
\q1
\v 22 Iniquities ensnare a man, 
\q2 and every one is bound in the chains of his own sins. 
\q1
\v 23 Such a man dies with the uninstructed; 
\q2 and he is cast forth from the abundance of his own livelihood, 
\q2 and has perished through folly. 
\c 6 
\p
\q1
\v 1 \add My\add* son, if you become surety for your friend,
\q2  you will deliver your hand to an enemy. 
\q1
\v 2 For a man's own lips become a strong snare to him, 
\q2 and he is caught with the lips of his own mouth. 
\q1
\v 3 \add My\add* son, do what I command you, and deliver yourself; 
\q2 for because of your friend you have come into the hands of evil \add men\add*: 
\q2 go; don't grow weary, but stir up your friend too, for whom you have become surety. 
\q1
\v 4 Don't give sleep to your eyes, 
\q2 nor let your eyelids drowse; 
\q1
\v 5 that you may deliver yourself as a doe out of the snare, 
\q2 and as a bird out of a trap. 
\p
\q1
\v 6 Go to the ant, O sluggard; 
\q2 and see, and emulate his ways, and become wiser than he. 
\q1
\v 7 For because he has no field, 
\q2 nor any one to compel him, 
\q2 and is under no master, 
\q1
\v 8 he prepares his food in the summer, 
\q2 and makes abundant provision in harvest. 
\q1
\v 8a Or go to the bee, and learn how she is a worker, 
\q2 and how noble she makes her work; 
\q1
\v 8b whose labours kings and private people use for health, 
\q2 and she is desired and respected by all: 
\q1
\v 8c though weak in strength, 
\q2 she has advanced by honouring wisdom. 
\q1
\v 9 How long will you lie, O sluggard? 
\q2 And when will you awake out of sleep? 
\q1
\v 10 You sleep a little, and sit a little, 
\q2 and doze a short while, 
\q2 and fold your arms over your breast a little, 
\q1
\v 11 then poverty comes upon you like an evil traveller, 
\q2 and need \add comes\add* like a swift runner: 
\q1
\v 11a but if you are diligent, your harvest will come like a wellspring, 
\q2 and need will flee like a bad runner. 
\p
\q1
\v 12 A foolish and lawless man goes in ways that are not good. 
\q1
\v 13 He winks with the eye, and makes a sign with his foot, 
\q2 and signals with the gestures of his fingers. 
\q1
\v 14 \add His\add* perverse heart devises evils: 
\q2 at all times such a man stirs up troubles to a city. 
\q1
\v 15 Therefore his destruction will come suddenly; 
\q2 overthrow and irretrievable ruin. 
\p
\q1
\v 16 For he rejoices in all things which God hates, 
\q2 and he is broken because of impurity of soul: 
\q1
\v 17 A haughty eye, an unjust tongue, 
\q2 hands shedding the blood of the righteous; 
\q1
\v 18 and a heart devising evil thoughts, 
\q2 and feet hastening to do evil. 
\q1
\v 19 An unjust witness kindles lies, 
\q2 and sends disputes among brothers. 
\p
\q1
\v 20 \add My\add* son, keep the laws of your father, 
\q2 and don't reject the ordinances of your mother: 
\q1
\v 21 but bind them upon your soul continually, 
\q2 and hang them as a chain about your neck. 
\q1
\v 22 Whenever you walk, take it with you, and let it be with you; 
\q2 and when you sleep let it keep you; 
\q2 that it may talk with you when you wake. 
\q1
\v 23 For the commandment of the law is a lamp and a light; 
\q2 and reproof and instruction are the way of life: 
\q1
\v 24 to keep you continually from a married woman, 
\q2 and from the slanderous tongue of another tongue. 
\p
\q1
\v 25 Don't let the desire of beauty overcome you, 
\q2 nor be caught by your eyes, nor be captivated with her eyelids. 
\q1
\v 26 For the value of a prostitute is as much as of one loaf; 
\q2 and a woman hunts for the precious souls of men. 
\q1
\v 27 Will any one bind fire in his bosom, 
\q2 and not burn his garments? 
\q1
\v 28 or will any one walk on coals of fire, 
\q2 and not burn his feet? 
\q1
\v 29 So is he who goes in to a married woman; 
\q2 he will not be held guiltless, 
\q2 neither any one that touches her. 
\q1
\v 30 It is no wonder if someone is caught stealing, 
\q2 for he steals to satisfy himself when he is hungry:
\q1
\v 31 but if he should be taken, he will repay sevenfold, 
\q2 and will deliver himself by giving all his goods. 
\q1
\v 32 But the adulterer through lack of sense procures destruction to his soul. 
\q1
\v 33 He endures both pain and disgrace, 
\q2 and his reproach will never be wiped off. 
\q1
\v 34 For her husband's heart is full of jealousy: 
\q2 he will not spare in the day of vengeance. 
\q1
\v 35 He will not exchange \add his\add* enmity for any ransom: 
\q2 neither will he be reconciled by many gifts. 
\c 7 
\p
\q1
\v 1 \add My\add* son, keep my words, 
\q2 and hide my commandments with you. 
\q1
\v 1a \add My\add* son, honour the Lord, 
\q2 and you will be strong; and fear none but him: 
\q1
\v 2 keep my commandments, and you will live; 
\q2 and \add keep\add* my words like the pupils of \add your\add* eyes. 
\q1
\v 3 Bind them on your fingers, 
\q2 and write \add them\add* on the breadth of your heart. 
\p
\q1
\v 4 Say to wisdom, You are my sister, 
\q2 and make prudence your familiar friend;
\q1
\v 5 that she may keep you from a strange and wicked woman, 
\q2 if she should assail you with flattering words. 
\p
\q1
\v 6 For leaning out from a window of her house into the streets, 
\q2 whenever she sees among the foolish children 
\q2 a young man lacking sense, 
\q1
\v 7 passing by the corner in the passages \f + \fr 7:7 \fqa Gr. \ft of.\f*near her house, 
\q1
\v 8 and talking in the evening darkness,
\q1
\v 9 when there happens \add to be\add* the stillness of night and of darkness: 
\q1
\v 10 and the woman meets him having the appearance of a prostitute, 
\q2 that causes the hearts of young men to flutter. 
\q1
\v 11 She is restless, and debauched, 
\q2 and her feet abide not at home. 
\q1
\v 12 For at one time she wanders outside, 
\q2 and at \add another\add* time she lies in wait in the streets, at every corner. 
\q1
\v 13 Then she caught him, and kissed him, 
\q2 and with an impudent face said to him, 
\q1
\v 14 I have a peace-offering; 
\q2 today I pay my vows: 
\q1
\v 15 therefore I came forth to meet you, desiring your face; 
\q2 I have found you. 
\q1
\v 16 I have spread my bed with coverings, 
\q2 and I have covered it with double tapestry from Egypt. 
\q1
\v 17 I have sprinkled my couch with saffron, 
\q2 and my house with cinnamon. 
\q1
\v 18 Come, and let us enjoy love until the morning; 
\q2 come, and let us roll ourselves in love. 
\q1
\v 19 For my husband isn't at home, 
\q2 but is gone on a long journey, 
\q1
\v 20 having taken in his hand a bundle of money: 
\q2 after many days he will return to his house. 
\p
\q1
\v 21 So with much talk she prevailed on him to go astray, 
\q2 and with the snares of her lips she shipwrecked him. 
\q1
\v 22 He followed her, being bewildered, 
\q2 and as an ox is led to the slaughter, and as a dog to bonds, 
\q2 or as a hart shot in the liver with an arrow: 
\q1
\v 23 and he hastens like a bird into a snare, 
\q2 not knowing that he is running for \add his\add* life. 
\p
\q1
\v 24 Now then, \add my\add* son, listen to me, 
\q2 and pay attention to the words of my mouth. 
\q1
\v 25 Don't let your heart turn aside to her ways:
\q1
\v 26 for she has wounded and cast down many, 
\q2 and those whom she has killed are innumerable. 
\q1
\v 27 Her house is the ways of Hades, 
\q2 leading down to the chambers of death. 
\c 8 
\p
\q1
\v 1 You will proclaim wisdom, 
\q2 that prudence may obey you. 
\q1
\v 2 For she is on lofty eminences, 
\q2 and stands among the ways. 
\q1
\v 3 For she sits by the gates of princes, 
\q2 and is sung at the entrances, 
\q1
\v 4 You, O men, I exhort; 
\q2 and utter my voice to the sons of men. 
\q1
\v 5 O you naive, understand subtlety, 
\q2 and you who are untaught, take it to heart. 
\q1
\v 6 Listen to me; for I will speak solemn \add truths\add*; 
\q2 and will bring forth right things from my lips. 
\q1
\v 7 For my throat will meditate truth; 
\q2 and false lips are an abomination before me. 
\q1
\v 8 All the words of my mouth are in righteousness; 
\q2 there is nothing in them wrong or perverse. 
\q1
\v 9 They are all evident to those who understand, 
\q2 and right to those who find knowledge. 
\q1
\v 10 Receive instruction, and not silver; 
\q2 and knowledge rather than tried gold. 
\q1
\v 11 For wisdom is better than precious stones; 
\q2 and no precious thing is worthy of it. 
\p
\q1
\v 12 I, wisdom, have dwelt \add with\add* counsel, 
\q2 and I have summoned knowledge and understanding. 
\q1
\v 13 The fear of the Lord hates unrighteousness, and insolence, and pride, and the ways of wicked men; 
\q2 and I hate the perverse ways of evil men. 
\q1
\v 14 Counsel and safety are mine: 
\q2 prudence is mine, and strength is mine. 
\q1
\v 15 By me kings reign, 
\q2 and princes write justice. 
\q1
\v 16 By me nobles become great, 
\q2 and rulers by me govern the earth. 
\q1
\v 17 I love those who love me; 
\q2 and those who seek me will find \add me\add*. 
\p
\q1
\v 18 Wealth and glory belong to me; 
\q2 yes, abundant possessions and righteousness. 
\q1
\v 19 \add It is\add* better to have my fruit than gold and precious stones; 
\q2 and my produce is better than choice silver. 
\q1
\v 20 I walk in ways of righteousness, 
\q2 and move among the paths of judgment; 
\q1
\v 21 that I may apportion possessions to those who love me, 
\q2 and may fill their treasures with good things. 
\q1
\v 21a If I declare to you the things that daily happen, 
\q2 I will remember \add also\add* to recount the things from of old. 
\p
\q1
\v 22 The Lord created me as the beginning of his ways, for his works. 
\q1
\v 23 He established me \f + \fr 8:23 \fqa Gr. \ft before the age.\f*before time \add was\add* in the beginning, 
\q2 before he made the earth; 
\q1
\v 24 even before he made the depths, 
\q2 before the fountains of water came forth: 
\q1
\v 25 before the mountains were settled, 
\q2 and before all hills, he begets me. 
\q1
\v 26 The Lord made lands and uninhabited \add tracts\add*, 
\q2 and the highest inhabited parts of the world. 
\q1
\v 27 When he prepared the heaven, I was present with him; 
\q2 and when he \f + \fr 8:27 \fqa Or, \ft marked out.\f*prepared his throne upon the winds: 
\q1
\v 28 and when he strengthened the clouds above; 
\q2 and when he secured the fountains beneath heaven; 
\q1
\v 29 and when he strengthened the foundations of the earth, 
\q1
\v 30 I was by him, fitting all things together; 
\q2 I was the one in whom he delighted,
\q2 and daily I rejoiced in his presence continually, 
\q1
\v 31 when he rejoiced after completing the world, 
\q2 and rejoiced among the children of men. 
\p
\q1
\v 32 Now then, \add my\add* son, hear me: 
\q2 blessed is the man who listens to me, 
\q2 and the mortal who keeps my ways;
\q1
\v 34 watching daily at my doors, 
\q2 waiting at the posts of my entrances. 
\q1
\v 35 For my ways are the ways of life, 
\q2 and favour from the Lord is prepared. 
\q1
\v 36 But those who sin against me act wickedly against their own souls: 
\q2 and those who hate me love death. 
\c 9 
\p
\q1
\v 1 Wisdom has built a house for herself, 
\q2 and set up seven pillars. 
\q1
\v 2 She has killed her beasts; 
\q2 she has mingled her wine in a bowl, and prepared her table. 
\q1
\v 3 She has sent her servants, 
\q2 calling with a loud proclamation over the bowl, saying, 
\q1
\v 4 Whoever is foolish, let him turn aside to me: 
\q2 and to those who lack understanding she says, 
\q1
\v 5 Come, eat of my bread, 
\q2 and drink wine which I have mingled for you. 
\p
\q1
\v 6 Leave folly, and you will live; 
\q2 and seek prudence, and improve understanding by knowledge. 
\q1
\v 7 He who reproves evil \add men\add* will bring dishonour on himself; 
\q2 and he who rebukes an ungodly \add man\add* will disgrace himself. 
\q1
\v 8 Don't rebuke evil \add men\add*, lest they hate you: 
\q2 rebuke a wise \add man\add*, and he will love you. 
\q1
\v 9 Give an opportunity to a wise \add man\add*, and he will be wiser: 
\q2 instruct a just man, and he will receive more \add instruction\add*. 
\q1
\v 10 The fear of the Lord is the \f + \fr 9:10 \fqa Or, \ft summit.\f*beginning of wisdom, 
\q2 and the counsel of holy ones is understanding: 
\q1
\v 10a for to know the law is the mark of a good understanding. 
\q1
\v 11 For in this way you will live long, 
\q2 and years of your life will be added to you. 
\p
\q1
\v 12 Son, if you are wise for yourself, you will also be wise for your neighbours; 
\q2 but if you prove wicked, you alone will draw up evils. 
\q1
\v 12a He who stays himself upon lies will shepherd the winds, 
\q2 and he will pursue birds in their flight: 
\q1
\v 12b for he has forsaken the ways of his own vineyard, 
\q2 and has gone astray from the tracks of his own field; 
\q1
\v 12c and he goes through a dry desert, 
\q2 and a \add land\add* appointed to drought, 
\q2 and he gathers barrenness with his hands. 
\p
\q1
\v 13 A foolish and bold woman, 
\q2 who doesn't know shame, 
\q2 comes to want a morsel. 
\q1
\v 14 She sits at the doors of her house, 
\q2 on a seat openly in the streets, 
\q1
\v 15 calling to passers by, 
\q2 and to those who go straight on their ways: 
\q1
\v 16 Whoever is most senseless of you, let him turn aside to me; 
\q2 and I exhort those who lack prudence, saying, 
\q1
\v 17 Take and enjoy secret bread, 
\q2 and the sweet water of theft. 
\p
\q1
\v 18 But he does not know that the earthborn perish beside her, 
\q2 and meets upon the threshold of Hades. 
\q1
\v 18a But hasten away, 
\q2 don't delay in the place, 
\q2 nor fix your eye upon her: 
\q1
\v 18b for thus will you pass through foreign water;
\q2 and cross over a foreign river;
\q1
\v 18c but abstain from foreign water, 
\q2 and don't drink from a foreign fountain, 
\q1
\v 18d that you may live long, 
\q2 and years of life may be added to you. 
\c 10 
\q1
\v 1 A wise son makes \add his\add* father glad: 
\q2 but a foolish son is a grief to his mother. 
\q1
\v 2 Treasures will not profit the lawless: 
\q2 but righteousness will deliver from death. 
\q1
\v 3 The Lord will not starve a righteous soul: 
\q2 but he will overthrow the life of the ungodly. 
\q1
\v 4 Poverty brings a man low: 
\q2 but capable hands make rich. 
\q1
\v 4a A son who is instructed will be wise, 
\q2 and will use the fool for a servant. 
\q1
\v 5 A prudent son is preserved from the heat: 
\q2 but a lawless son is blighted by the winds in harvest.
\q1
\v 6 The blessing of the Lord is upon the head of the just: 
\q2 but untimely grief will cover the mouth of the ungodly. 
\q1
\v 7 The memory of the just is praised; 
\q2 but the name of the ungodly \add man\add* is extinguished. 
\q1
\v 8 The wise in heart will receive commandments; 
\q2 but he who is unguarded in his lips and speaks crookedly will be overthrown. 
\q1
\v 9 He who walks straightforwardly, walks confidently; 
\q2 but he who perverts his ways will be known. 
\q1
\v 10 He who winks with his eyes deceitfully, brings sorrows on men; 
\q2 but he who reproves boldly is a peacemaker. 
\q1
\v 11 \add There is\add* a fountain of life in the hand of a righteous man; 
\q2 but destruction will cover the mouth of the ungodly. 
\q1
\v 12 Hatred stirs up strife; 
\q2 but friendship covers all who do not love strife. 
\q1
\v 13 He who brings forth wisdom from his lips smites the \f + \fr 10:13 \fqa Lit. \ft heartless man, \fqa Hebraism.\f*fool with a rod. 
\q1
\v 14 The wise will hide discretion; 
\q2 but the mouth of the hasty draws near to ruin. 
\q1
\v 15 The wealth of rich men is a strong city; 
\q2 but poverty is the ruin of the ungodly. 
\q1
\v 16 The works of the righteous produce life; 
\q2 but the fruits of the ungodly \add produce\add* sins. 
\q1
\v 17 Instruction guards the right ways of life; 
\q2 but uncorrected instruction goes astray. 
\q1
\v 18 Righteous lips cover enmity; 
\q2 but those who utter insults are most foolish. 
\q1
\v 19 By a multitude of words you will not escape sin; 
\q2 but if you restrain your lips you will be prudent. 
\q1
\v 20 The tongue of the just is refined silver; 
\q2 but the heart of the ungodly will fail. 
\q1
\v 21 The lips of the righteous know sublime \add truths\add*: 
\q2 but the foolish die in want. 
\q1
\v 22 The blessing of the Lord is upon the head of the righteous; 
\q2 it enriches \add him\add*, and grief of heart will not be added to \add it\add*. 
\q1
\v 23 A fool does mischief for fun; 
\q2 but wisdom gives birth to prudence in a man. 
\q1
\v 24 The ungodly is engulfed in destruction; 
\q2 but the desire of the righteous is acceptable. 
\q1
\v 25 When the storm passes by, the ungodly vanishes away; 
\q2 but the righteous, having turned aside, is saved forever. 
\q1
\v 26 As a sour grape is hurtful to the teeth, and smoke to the eyes, 
\q2 so wickedness hurts those who practise it. 
\q1
\v 27 The fear of the Lord adds \add length\add* of days: 
\q2 but the years of the ungodly will be shortened. 
\q1
\v 28 Joy rests long with the righteous: 
\q2 but the hope of the ungodly will perish. 
\q1
\v 29 The fear of the Lord is a stronghold for the holy one: 
\q2 but ruin \add comes\add* to those who work evil. 
\q1
\v 30 The righteous will never give way: 
\q2 but the ungodly will not dwell in the earth. 
\q1
\v 31 The mouth of the righteous drops wisdom: 
\q2 but the tongue of the unjust will perish. 
\q1
\v 32 The lips of just men drop grace: 
\q2 but the mouth of the ungodly is perverse. 
\c 11 
\q1
\v 1 False balances are an abomination before the Lord:
\q2 but a just weight is acceptable to him. 
\q1
\v 2 Wherever pride enters, there will also be disgrace: 
\q2 but the mouth of the lowly meditates wisdom. 
\q1
\v 3 When a just man dies he leaves regret: 
\q2 but the destruction of the ungodly is speedy, and causes joy. 
\q1
\v 5 Righteousness makes straight the paths of the blameless;
\q2 but ungodliness falls into injustice.
\q1
\v 6 The righteousness of upright men delivers them: 
\q2 but transgressors are caught in their own destruction. 
\q1
\v 7 At the death of a just man his hope does not perish: 
\q2 but the boast of the ungodly perishes. 
\q1
\v 8 A righteous man escapes from a snare, 
\q2 and the ungodly man is delivered up in his place. 
\q1
\v 9 In the mouth of the ungodly is a snare for citizens;
\q2 but the understanding of the righteous is a safe guide.
\q1
\v 10 In the prosperity of righteous men a city prospers: 
\q1
\v 11 but by the mouths of ungodly men it is overthrown. 
\q1
\v 12 A man void of understanding sneers at \add his fellow\add* citizens: 
\q2 but a sensible man is quiet. 
\q1
\v 13 A double-tongued man discloses the \add secret\add* counsels of an assembly: 
\q2 but he who is faithful in spirit conceals matters. 
\q1
\v 14 Those who have no \f + \fr 11:14 \fqa Or, \ft governance.\f*guidance fall like leaves: 
\q2 but in much counsel there is safety. 
\q1
\v 15 A wicked man does harm when he meets a just man: 
\q2 and he hates the sound of safety. 
\q1
\v 16 A gracious wife \f + \fr 11:16 \fqa Gr. \ft raises.\f*brings glory to her husband: 
\q2 but a woman hating righteousness is a throne of dishonour. 
\q1 The slothful come to want: 
\q2 but the \f + \fr 11:16 \fqa Gr. \ft manly.\f*diligent support themselves with wealth. 
\q1
\v 17 A merciful man does good to his own soul: 
\q2 but the merciless destroys his own body. 
\q1
\v 18 An ungodly man performs unrighteous works: 
\q2 but the sowing of the righteous is a true reward. 
\q1
\v 19 A righteous son is born for life: 
\q2 but the persecution of the ungodly \add ends\add* in death. 
\q1
\v 20 Perverse ways are an abomination to the Lord: 
\q2 but all those who are blameless in their ways are acceptable to him. 
\q1
\v 21 He who joins hands unjustly will not be unpunished:
\q2 but he who sows righteousness will receive a sure reward.
\q1
\v 22 As an ornament in a pig's snout, 
\q2 so is beauty to an evil-minded woman. 
\q1
\v 23 All the desire of the righteous is good: 
\q2 but the hope of the ungodly will perish. 
\q1
\v 24 There are those who scatter what is their own, and gain more:
\q2 and there are those who gather, yet are diminished.
\q1
\v 25 Every sincere soul is blessed:
\q2 but a hot-tempered man is not respectable.
\q1
\v 26 May he who hoards grain leave it to the nations: 
\q2 but blessing be on the head of him who gives. 
\q1
\v 27 He who devises good \add counsels\add* seeks good favour: 
\q2 but \add as for\add* him that seeks after evil, it will overtake him. 
\q1
\v 28 He who trusts in wealth will fall; 
\q2 but he who supports the righteous will rise. 
\q1
\v 29 He who doesn't deal graciously with his own house will inherit the wind; 
\q2 and the fool will be servant to the wise man. 
\q1
\v 30 Out of the fruit of righteousness grows a tree of life; 
\q2 but the souls of transgressors are cut off before their time. 
\q1
\v 31 If the righteous is barely saved, 
\q2 where will the ungodly and the sinner appear? 
\c 12 
\q1
\v 1 He who loves instruction loves sense, 
\q2 but he who hates reproofs is a fool. 
\q1
\v 2 Better is he who has found favour with the Lord; 
\q2 but a transgressor will be passed over in silence. 
\q1
\v 3 A man will not prosper by wickedness; 
\q2 but the roots of the righteous will not be taken up. 
\q1
\v 4 A capable wife is a crown to her husband;
\q2 but as a worm in wood, so a wicked woman destroys her husband.
\q1
\v 5 The thoughts of the righteous \add are true\add* judgments; 
\q2 but ungodly men direct deceits. 
\q1
\v 6 The words of ungodly men are crafty; 
\q2 but the mouth of the upright will deliver them. 
\q1
\v 7 When the ungodly is overthrown, he vanishes away; 
\q2 but the houses of the just remain. 
\q1
\v 8 The mouth of an understanding \add man\add* is praised by men; 
\q2 but he who is dull of heart is mocked. 
\q1
\v 9 Better is a man in dishonour serving himself, 
\q2 than one honouring himself and lacking bread. 
\q1
\v 10 A righteous man has pity for the lives of his livestock;
\q2 but the compassion of the ungodly is merciless.
\q1
\v 11 He who tills his own land will be satisfied with bread; 
\q2 but those who pursue vanities are void of understanding. 
\q1
\v 11a He who enjoys himself in banquets of wine, 
\q2 will leave dishonour in his own strongholds. 
\q1
\v 12 The desires of the ungodly are evil; 
\q2 but the roots of the godly are in strong places. 
\q1
\v 13 For the sin of \add his\add* lips a sinner falls into snares; 
\q2 but a righteous man escapes from them. 
\q1
\v 13a He whose looks are gentle will be pitied, 
\q2 but he who contends in the gates will afflict souls. 
\q1
\v 14 The soul of a man will be filled with good from the fruits of his mouth; 
\q2 and the recompense of his lips will be given to him. 
\q1
\v 15 The ways of fools are right in their own eyes; 
\q2 but a wise man listens to counsels. 
\q1
\v 16 A fool declares his wrath the same day; 
\q2 but a prudent man hides his own disgrace. 
\q1
\v 17 A righteous man declares what is true; 
\q2 but an unjust witness is deceitful. 
\q1
\v 18 Some wound as they speak, \add like\add* swords; 
\q2 but the tongues of the wise heal. 
\q1
\v 19 True lips establish testimony; 
\q2 but a hasty witness has an unjust tongue. 
\q1
\v 20 \add There is\add* deceit in the heart of him who imagines evil; 
\q2 but those who desire peace will rejoice. 
\q1
\v 21 No injustice will please a just man; 
\q2 but the ungodly will be filled with mischief. 
\q1
\v 22 Lying lips are an abomination to the Lord; 
\q2 but he who deals faithfully is accepted with him. 
\q1
\v 23 An understanding man is a throne of knowledge; 
\q2 but the heart of fools will meet with curses. 
\q1
\v 24 The hand of chosen men will rule with ease; 
\q2 but the deceitful will be for a prey. 
\q1
\v 25 An anxious word troubles the heart of a righteous man; 
\q2 but a good message rejoices him. 
\q1
\v 26 A discerning righteous man will be a friend to himself;
\q2 but mischief will pursue sinners; 
\q2 and the way of ungodly men will lead them astray. 
\q1
\v 27 A deceitful man will catch no game; 
\q2 but a \f + \fr 12:27 \fqa Gr. \ft pure.\f*blameless man is a precious possession. 
\q1
\v 28 In the ways of righteousness is life; 
\q2 but the ways of those who remember injuries \add lead\add* to death. 
\c 13 
\q1
\v 1 A prudent son is obedient to his father: 
\q2 but a disobedient son will be destroyed. 
\q1
\v 2 A good \add man\add* will eat of the fruits of righteousness: 
\q2 but the lives of transgressors will perish before their time. 
\q1
\v 3 He who guards his own mouth keeps his own life: 
\q2 but he who is hasty with his lips will bring terror upon himself. 
\q1
\v 4 Every slothful man desires, 
\q2 but the hands of the diligent are active. 
\q1
\v 5 A righteous man hates an unjust word: 
\q2 but an ungodly man is ashamed, and will have no confidence.
\q1
\v 6 Righteousness guards the blameless;
\q2 but sin makes the ungodly vile.
\q1
\v 7 There are those who, having nothing, make themselves rich;
\q2 and there are those who humble themselves while having much wealth.
\q1
\v 8 A man's own wealth is the ransom of his life:
\q2 but a poor man does not face threats.
\q1
\v 9 The righteous always have light: 
\q2 but the light of the ungodly is quenched. 
\q1
\v 9a Crafty souls go astray in sins: 
\q2 but just men pity, and are merciful. 
\q1
\v 10 A wicked man does wickedness with insolence: 
\q2 but those who know themselves are wise. 
\q1
\v 11 Wealth gained in haste with lawlessness is diminished: 
\q2 but he who gathers for himself with godliness will be increased. 
\q2 The righteous man is merciful, and lends. 
\q1
\v 12 Better is he who begins to help heartily, 
\q2 than he who promises and leads \add another\add* to hope: 
\q2 for a good desire is a tree of life. 
\q1
\v 13 He who slights a matter will be slighted by it: 
\q2 but he who fears the commandment will be sound. 
\q1
\v 13a To a crafty son there will be nothing good: 
\q2 but a wise servant will have prosperous doings, 
\q2 and his way will be directed aright. 
\q1
\v 14 The law of the wise is a fountain of life: 
\q2 but the man void of understanding will die by a snare. 
\q1
\v 15 Sound discretion gives favour, 
\q2 and to know the law belongs to a sound mind; 
\q2 but the ways of scorners tend to destruction. 
\q1
\v 16 Every prudent man acts with knowledge: 
\q2 but the fool displays his own mischief. 
\q1
\v 17 A rash king will fall into mischief: 
\q2 but a wise messenger will deliver him. 
\q1
\v 18 Instruction removes poverty and disgrace: 
\q2 but he who attends to reproofs will be honoured. 
\q1
\v 19 The desires of the godly gladden the soul, 
\q2 but the works of the ungodly are far from knowledge. 
\q1
\v 20 If you walk with wise men you will be wise: 
\q2 but he who walks with fools will become known. 
\q1
\v 21 Evil will pursue sinners; 
\q2 but good will overtake the righteous. 
\q1
\v 22 A good man will inherit children's children; 
\q2 and the wealth of ungodly men is laid up for the just. 
\q1
\v 23 The righteous will live many years in wealth: 
\q2 but the unrighteous will perish suddenly. 
\q1
\v 24 He who spares the rod hates his son: 
\q2 but he who loves, carefully chastens \add him\add*. 
\q1
\v 25 A just \add man\add* eats and satisfies his soul: 
\q2 but the souls of the ungodly are in want. 
\c 14 
\q1
\v 1 Wise women build houses: 
\q2 but a foolish one tears them down with her hands. 
\q1
\v 2 He who walks uprightly fears the Lord; 
\q2 but he who is perverse in his ways will be dishonoured. 
\q1
\v 3 Out of the mouth of fools \add comes\add* a rod of pride; 
\q2 but the lips of the wise preserve them. 
\q1
\v 4 Where there are no oxen, the stalls are clean; 
\q2 but where there is abundant produce, the strength of the ox is evident. 
\q1
\v 5 A faithful witness does not lie; 
\q2 but an unjust witness kindles lies. 
\q1
\v 6 You will seek wisdom with wicked men, and will not find it; 
\q2 but discretion is easily available with the prudent. 
\q1
\v 7 All things are against a foolish man; 
\q2 but wise lips are the weapons of discretion. 
\q1
\v 8 The wisdom of the prudent will understand their ways; 
\q2 but the folly of fools is led astray. 
\q1
\v 9 The houses of transgressors will need purification; 
\q2 but the houses of the just are acceptable. 
\q1
\v 10 A discerning heart is a sorrowful soul;
\q2 but when it rejoices, it is not mingled with arrogance.
\q1
\v 11 The houses of ungodly men will be completely destroyed; 
\q2 but the tents of those who walk uprightly will stand. 
\q1
\v 12 There is a way which seems right to men, 
\q2 but its ends lead down to the depths of Hades. 
\q1
\v 13 Grief doesn't mingle with mirth; 
\q2 and joy in the end comes to grief. 
\q1
\v 14 A self-willed \add man\add* will be filled with his own ways; 
\q2 but a good man with his own thoughts. 
\q1
\v 15 The innocent believes every word: 
\q2 but the prudent man thinks twice. 
\q1
\v 16 A wise man fears, and departs from evil; 
\q2 but the fool trusts in himself, and joins himself with a transgressor. 
\q1
\v 17 A quick-tempered man acts without counsel;
\q2 but a sensible man endures many things.
\q1
\v 18 Fools inherit wickedness;
\q2 but the prudent hold fast to understanding.
\q1
\v 19 Evil men will fall before the good;
\q2 and the ungodly will wait at the doors of the righteous.
\q1
\v 20 Friends will hate poor friends; 
\q2 but the friends of the rich are many. 
\q1
\v 21 He who dishonours the needy sins: 
\q2 but he who has pity on the poor is most blessed. 
\q1
\v 22 Those who go astray devise evils: 
\q2 but the good devise mercy and truth. 
\q1 The framers of evil do not understand mercy and truth: 
\q2 but compassion and faithfulness are with the framers of good. 
\q1
\v 23 In every careful man there is abundance: 
\q2 but the pleasure-taking and indolent will be in need. 
\q1
\v 24 Prudence is the crown of the wise: 
\q2 but the occupation of fools is evil. 
\q1
\v 25 A faithful witness will deliver a soul from evil: 
\q2 but a deceitful \add man\add* kindles lies. 
\q1
\v 26 In the fear of the Lord is strong confidence: 
\q2 and he leaves a support for his children. 
\q1
\v 27 The commandment of the Lord is a fountain of life; 
\q2 and it causes \add men\add* to turn aside from the snare of death. 
