\q1
\v 1 A good name is better than much wealth,
\q2 and good favour is above silver and gold. 
\q1
\v 2 The rich and the poor meet together; 
\q2 but the Lord made them both. 
\q1
\v 3 An intelligent man, seeing evil punished severely, is himself instructed, 
\q2 but fools pass by and are punished. 
\q1
\v 4 The fear of the Lord is the fruit of wisdom, 
\q2 and wealth, and glory, and life. 
\q1
\v 5 Thistles and snares are in perverse ways; 
\q2 but he who keeps his soul will keep away from them. 
\q1
\v 7 The rich will rule over the poor, 
\q2 and servants will lend to their own masters. 
\q1
\v 8 He who sows wickedness will reap troubles; 
\q2 and will fully receive the punishment of his deeds. 
\q1
\v 8a God loves a cheerful and liberal man; 
\q2 but he will bring the vanity of his works to completion. 
\q1
\v 9 He who has pity on the poor will himself be maintained; 
\q2 for he has given of his own bread to the poor. 
\q1
\v 9a He who gives liberally secures victory and honour; 
\q2 but he takes away the life of those who possess. 
\q1
\v 10 Cast out a pestilent person from the council, and strife will go out with him; 
\q2 for when he sits in the council he dishonours all. 
\q1
\v 11 The Lord loves holy hearts, 
\q2 and all blameless persons are acceptable with him. 
\q1 A king shepherds with his lips; 
\q1
\v 12 but the eyes of the Lord preserve discretion; 
\q2 but the transgressor despises \add wise\add* words. 
\q1
\v 13 The sluggard makes excuses, and says, \add There is\add* a lion in the ways, 
\q2 and murderers in the streets. 
\q1
\v 14 The mouth of a transgressor is a deep pit; 
\q2 and he who is hated by the Lord will fall into it. 
\q1
\v 14a Evil ways are before a man, and he does not like to turn away from them; 
\q2 but it is needful to turn aside from a perverse and bad way. 
\q1
\v 15 Folly is attached to the heart of a child, 
\q2 but the rod and instruction are far from him. 
\q1
\v 16 He who oppresses the poor, increases his own substance, 
\q2 and gives to the rich, only to diminish it.
\p
\q1
\v 17 Incline your ear to the words of wise men: 
\q2 hear also my word, and apply your heart, 
\q1
\v 18 that you may know that they are good: 
\q2 and if you lay them to heart, they will gladden you upon your lips. 
\q1
\v 19 That your hope may be in the Lord, 
\q2 and he may make his way known to you. 
\q1
\v 20 You, too, record them for yourself three times on the table of your heart, 
\q2 for counsel and knowledge. 
\q1
\v 21 I therefore teach you truth, and good knowledge to hear; 
\q2 that you may answer words of truth to those who question you. 
\p
\q1
\v 22 Do no violence to the poor, for he is needy: 
\q2 neither dishonour the helpless \add man\add* in the gates. 
\q1
\v 23 For the Lord will plead his cause, 
\q2 and he will rescue your soul unharmed. 
\p
\q1
\v 24 Don't be companion to a hot-tempered man; 
\q2 neither lodge with a quick-tempered man: 
\q1
\v 25 lest you learn of his ways, 
\q2 and get snares to your soul.
\b
\q1
\v 26 Don't become surety from respect of a man's person. 
\q1
\v 27 For if you have nothing with which to repay,
\q2 they will take the bed \add that is\add* under you. 
\q1
\v 28 Don't remove the old \f + \fr 22:28 \fqa Gr. \ft eternal.\f*landmarks, which your fathers placed. 
\q1
\v 29 An observant man, sharp in his work, ought to stand before kings, 
\q2 and not stand before slothful men. 
\c 23 
\p
\q1
\v 1 If you sit to dine at the table of rulers,
\q2 carefully consider the things set before you;
\q1
\v 2 and put forth your hand, knowing
\q2 that you must prepare such things yourself.
\q1
\v 3 But if you are insatiable,
\q2 do not desire his provisions;
\q2 for these belong to a false life.
\p
\q1
\v 4 If you are poor, don't overextend yourself for a rich man; 
\q2 but hold back according to your own judgment. 
\q1
\v 5 If you should fix your eye upon it, it will be nowhere to be seen; 
\q2 for wings like an eagle's have been prepared for it, 
\q2 and it returns to the house of its former owner.
\p
\q1
\v 6 Don't dine with an grudging man, 
\q2 nor desire his food: 
\q1
\v 7 for as one who swallows a hair,
\q2 so he eats and drinks. 
\q1 Do not bring him into your company, 
\q2 nor eat your morsel with him: 
\q1
\v 8 for he will vomit it up, 
\q2 and spoil your fair words. 
\p
\q1
\v 9 Say nothing in the ears of a fool, 
\q2 lest at any time he sneer at your wise words.
\p
\q1
\v 10 Don't remove the ancient landmarks; 
\q2 and don't enter upon the possession of the fatherless: 
\q1
\v 11 for the Lord is their redeemer; 
\q2 he is mighty, and will plead their cause with you. 
\p
\q1
\v 12 Apply your heart to instruction, 
\q2 and prepare your ears for words of discretion. 
\p
\q1
\v 13 Don't refrain from chastening a child; 
\q2 for if you beat him with the rod, he will not die. 
\q1
\v 14 For you will beat him with the rod, 
\q2 and will deliver his soul from death. 
\p
\q1
\v 15 Son, if your heart be wise, 
\q2 you will also gladden my heart; 
\q1
\v 16 and your lips will converse with my lips, 
\q2 if they be right. 
\q1
\v 17 Don't let your heart envy sinners: 
\q2 but be in the fear of the Lord all day long. 
\q1
\v 18 For if you should keep these things, 
\q2 you will have posterity; 
\q2 and your hope will not be removed. 
\p
\q1
\v 19 Hear, \add my\add* son, and be wise, 
\q2 and rightly direct the thoughts of your heart. 
\q1
\v 20 Don't be a wine drinker, 
\q2 nor devote yourself to feasts, 
\q2 and the buying of meat: 
\q1
\v 21 for every drunkard and debauchee will be poor; 
\q2 and every drowsy man will clothe himself with tatters and ragged garments. 
\p
\q1
\v 22 Listen, \add my\add* son, to your father who fathered you,
\q2 and don't despise \add your mother\add* because she has grown old. 
\q1
\v 24 A righteous father brings up \add his children\add* well; 
\q2 and his soul rejoices over a wise son. 
\q1
\v 25 Let your father and your mother rejoice over you, 
\q2 and let her who bore you be glad. 
\p
\q1
\v 26 \add My\add* son, give me your heart, 
\q2 and let your eyes observe my ways. 
\q1
\v 27 For a strange house is a vessel full of holes; 
\q2 and a strange well is narrow. 
\q1
\v 28 For such a one will perish suddenly; 
\q2 and every transgressor will be consumed. 
\p
\q1
\v 29 Who \add has\add* woe? 
\q2 Who \add has\add* trouble? 
\q2 Who \add has\add* quarrels? 
\q2 Who \add has\add* vexations and disputes? 
\q2 Who \add has\add* bruises without a cause? 
\q2 Whose eyes are livid? 
\q1
\v 30 Are they not those of men who linger over wine? 
\q2 Those who track down where drinking parties are held? 
\q2 Don't be drunk with wine; 
\q2 but converse with just men, and converse \add with them\add* \f + \fr 23:30 \fqa Gr. \ft in public walks.\f*openly. 
\q1
\v 31 For if you should set your eyes on bowls and cups, 
\q2 you will afterwards walk barer than a pestle. 
\q1
\v 32 But at last \add such a one\add* stretches himself out as one struck by a snake, 
\q2 and venom is poured into him as by a horned snake. 
\q1
\v 33 Whenever your eyes behold a strange woman,
\q2 then your mouth will speak perverse things. 
\q1
\v 34 You will lie down as in the heart of the sea, 
\q2 and like a helmsman in a great swell. 
\q1
\v 35 You will say, They struck me, and I was not pained; 
\q2 and they abused me, and I didn't know it: 
\q2 when will it be morning, 
\q2 that I may go and seek those with whom I may gather again? 
\c 24 
\p
\q1
\v 1 \add My\add* son, don't envy evil men,
\q2 nor desire to be with them. 
\q1
\v 2 For their heart meditates lies, 
\q2 and their lips speak troubles. 
\q1
\v 3 A house is built by wisdom, 
\q2 and is set up by understanding. 
\q1
\v 4 By discretion the chambers are filled 
\q2 with all precious and excellent wealth. 
\q1
\v 5 A wise man is better than a strong man; 
\q2 and a man who has prudence than a large estate. 
\q1
\v 6 War is carried on with generalship, 
\q2 and help comes with a counselling heart. 
\p
\q1
\v 7 Wisdom and good understanding are at the gates of the wise: 
\q2 the wise don't turn aside from the mouth of the Lord, 
\q2
\v 8 but deliberate in council. 
\q1 Death befalls uninstructed \add men\add*. 
\q1
\v 9 The fool also dies in sins; 
\q2 and uncleanness will defile a plague of a man 
\q2
\v 10 in an evil day and in the day of affliction,
\q2 until he comes to an end. 
\p
\q1
\v 11 Deliver those who are led away to death, 
\q2 and redeem those who are appointed to be killed; 
\q2 do not hold back. 
\q1
\v 12 But if you should say, I don't know this man; 
\q2 know that the Lord knows the hearts of all; 
\q2 and he who formed breath for all, he knows all things, 
\q2 who renders to every man according to his works. 
\q1
\v 13 \add My\add* son, eat honey, for the honeycomb is good, 
\q2 that your throat may be sweetened. 
\q1
\v 14 Thus will you perceive wisdom in your soul: 
\q2 for if you find it, your end will be good, 
\q2 and hope will not fail you. 
\p
\q1
\v 15 Don't bring an ungodly man into the pasture of the righteous: 
\q2 nor be deceived by the feeding of the belly. 
\q1
\v 16 For a righteous man will fall seven times, and rise \add again\add*: 
\q2 but the ungodly will be without strength in troubles. 
\q1
\v 17 If your enemy should fall, don't rejoice over him, 
\q2 neither be elated at his overthrow. 
\q1
\v 18 For the Lord will see \add it\add*, 
\q2 and it will not please him, 
\q2 and he will turn away his wrath from him. 
\q1
\v 19 Don't rejoice over evil-doers, 
\q2 neither be envious of sinners. 
\q1
\v 20 For there will be no offspring of the wicked;
\q2 and the light of the ungodly will be put out. 
\p
\q1
\v 21 \add My\add* son, fear God and the king; 
\q2 and do not disobey either of them. 
\q1
\v 22 For they will suddenly punish the ungodly, 
\q2 and who can know the vengeance \add inflicted\add* by both? 
\p
\q1
\v 22a \f + \fr 24:22a--22f \fqa Heb. \ft lacks these verses.\f*[A son that keeps the commandment will \f + \fr 24:22a \fqa Lit. \ft be outside of.\f*escape destruction; 
\q2 for \add such an one\add* has fully received it. 
\q1
\v 22b Let no falsehood be spoken to the king by the tongue; 
\q2 and let no falsehood proceed from his tongue. 
\q1
\v 22c The king's tongue is a sword, and not one of flesh; 
\q2 and whoever is given up to \add it\add* will be crushed: 
\q1
\v 22d for if his wrath is provoked, 
\q2 he destroys men with cords, 
\q1
\v 22e and devours men's bones, 
\q2 and burns them up as a flame, 
\q2 so that they are not \add even\add* fit to be eaten by the young eagles. 
\q1
\v 22f \add My\add* son, reverence my words, 
\q2 and receive them, and repent.
\q2 These things says the man to those who trust in God; and I cease.]
\b
\p
\q1
\v 22g \f + \fr 24:22g-22t \ft The following section corresponds to MT Proverbs 30:2–14, here placed after 24:22 in the Greek tradition.\f*For I am the most foolish of all men, 
\q2 and there isn't in me the wisdom of men. 
\q1
\v 22h God has taught me wisdom, 
\q2 and I know the knowledge of the holy. 
\q1
\v 22i Who has gone up to heaven, and come down? 
\q2 Who has gathered the winds in his bosom? 
\q2 Who has wrapped up the waters in a garment? 
\q2 Who has taken hold of all the ends of the earth? 
\q2 What is his name? Or what is the name of his children, that you may know? 
\q1
\v 22k For all the words of God are tried in the fire, 
\q2 and he defends those who reverence him. 
\q1
\v 22l Don't add to his words, lest he reprove you, 
\q2 and you be made a liar. 
\p
\q1
\v 22m Two things I ask of you; 
\q2 don't take your favour from me before I die. 
\q1
\v 22n Remove far from me vanity and falsehood: 
\q2 and don't give me wealth \add or \add* poverty; 
\q2 but assign me what is needful and sufficient: 
\q1
\v 22o lest I be filled and become false, and say, Who sees me? 
\q2 Or be poor and steal, and swear by the name of God. 
\p
\q1
\v 22p Don't deliver a servant into the hands of his master, 
\q2 lest he curse you, and you be completely destroyed. 
\q1
\v 22q A wicked offspring curses his father,
\q2 and does not bless his mother.
\q1
\v 22r A wicked offspring judges himself to be righteous, 
\q2 but has not washed his conduct. 
\q1
\v 22s A wicked offspring has lofty eyes, 
\q2 and exalts himself with his eyelids. 
\q1
\v 22t A wicked offspring has swords \add for \add* teeth 
\q2 and his molars are knives, 
\q2 so as to destroy and devour the lowly from the earth, 
\q2 and the poor from among men. 
\p
\q1
\v 23 These things I say to you who are wise \add for you\add* to understand: 
\q2 It isn't good to have respect of persons in judgment. 
\q1
\v 24 He who says of the ungodly, He is righteous,
\q2 will be cursed by peoples, and hated among the nations. 
\q1
\v 25 But those who reprove will appear more excellent, 
\q2 and blessing will come upon them; 
\q1
\v 26 and lips will kiss him who answers good words.
\q1
\v 27 Prepare your works for \add your\add* departure, 
\q2 and prepare yourself for the field; follow after me, 
\q2 and you will rebuild your house. 
\q1
\v 28 Don't be a false witness against your \add fellow\add* citizen, 
\q2 neither exaggerate with your lips. 
\q1
\v 29 Don't say, As he has treated me, so will I treat him, 
\q2 and I will avenge myself on him for the wrong he has done me. 
\q1
\v 30 A foolish man is like a farm, 
\q2 and a senseless man is like a vineyard. 
\q1
\v 31 If you let him alone, he will become altogether barren and covered with \f + \fr 24:31 \fqa Gr. \ft grass.\f*weeds; 
\q2 and it becomes abandoned, and its stone walls are broken down. 
\q1
\v 32 Afterwards I reflected, 
\q2 I looked that I might choose instruction. 
\q1
\v 33 I slumber a little, and I sleep a little, 
\q2 and for a little while I fold my arms across \add my\add* breast. 
\q1
\v 34 But if you do this, your poverty will come advancing before you; 
\q2 and your want like a swift runner. 
\p
\q1
\v 35 \f + \fr 24:35-62 \ft The following section corresponds to MT Proverbs 30:5–31:9, here placed after 24:34 in the Greek tradition.\f*The horse-leech had three dearly-beloved daughters: 
\q2 and these three did not satisfy her; 
\q2 and the fourth was not content to say, Enough. 
\q1
\v 36 Hades, and a woman's desire,
\q2 and Tartarus, and the earth not filled with water; 
\q2 and water and fire will never say, It is enough. 
\p
\q1
\v 37 The eye that laughs to scorn its father, 
\q2 and dishonours the old age of its mother: 
\q2 let the ravens of the valleys pick it out, 
\q2 and let the young eagles devour it. 
\q1
\v 38 Moreover there are three things impossible for me to comprehend, 
\q2 and the fourth I don't know: 
\q1
\v 39 the track of a flying eagle; 
\q2 and the ways of a snake on a rock; 
\q2 and the paths of a ship passing through the sea; 
\q2 and the ways of a man in youth. 
\q1
\v 40 Such is the way of an adulterous woman, 
\q2 who, having washed herself, 
\q2 says she has done nothing amiss. 
\p
\q1
\v 41 By three things the earth is troubled, 
\q2 and the fourth it cannot bear: 
\q1
\v 42 if a servant reigns; 
\q2 or a fool is filled with food; 
\q1
\v 43 or if a maid-servant casts out her own mistress; 
\q2 and if a hated woman gets a good husband. 
\p
\q1
\v 44 And \add there are\add* four very little things upon the earth, 
\q2 but these are wiser than the wise: 
\q1
\v 45 the ants which are weak, 
\q2 and \add yet \add* prepare \add their \add* food in summer; 
\q1
\v 46 the rock-badgers also \add are \add* a feeble race, 
\q2 who make their houses in the rocks. 
\q1
\v 47 The locusts have no king, 
\q2 and \add yet \add* march orderly at one command. 
\q1
\v 48 The lizard, which supports itself by \add its \add* hands, and is easily caught, 
\q2 dwells in the fortresses of kings. 
\p
\q1
\v 49 There are three things which go well, 
\q2 and a fourth which advances nobly. 
\q1
\v 50 A lion's cub, stronger than \add all other\add* beasts, 
\q2 which doesn't turn away, nor fear \add any \add* beast; 
\q1
\v 51 and a cock walking in boldly among the hens, 
\q2 and a goat leading the herd; 
\q2 and a king publicly addressing a nation. 
\p
\q1
\v 52 If you give yourself over to merriment, 
\q2 and stretch forth your hand in a quarrel, 
\q2 you will be disgraced. 
\q1
\v 53 Milk the milk, and there will be butter, 
\q2 and if you press the nostrils, blood will come out: 
\q2 so if you extort words, there will come forth quarrels and strifes. 
\p
\q1
\v 54 My words have been spoken by God—an oracle of a king, whom his mother instructed. 
\p
\q1
\v 55 What will you keep, my son? What? 
\q2 The words of God. 
\q2 My firstborn son, I speak to you: 
\q2 what, son of my womb? What, son of my vows? 
\q1
\v 56 Don't give your wealth to women, nor your mind and life to regret. 
\q2 Do all things with counsel: drink wine with counsel. 
\q1
\v 57 Rulers are prone to anger: 
\q2 therefore let them not drink wine: 
\q1
\v 58 lest they drink, and forget wisdom, 
\q2 and not be able to judge the weak rightly. 
\q1
\v 59 Give strong drink to those in sorrow, 
\q2 and wine to those in pain: 
\q1
\v 60 so they may forget their poverty, 
\q2 and may not remember their troubles any more. 
\q1
\v 61 Open your mouth with the word of God, 
\q2 and judge all fairly. 
\q1
\v 62 Open your mouth and judge justly, 
\q2 and decide for the poor and weak. 
\c 25 
\p
\v 1 These are the additional instructions of Solomon, which the friends of Hezekiah king of Judea copied out. 
\b
\q1
\v 2 The glory of God conceals a thing:
\q2 but the glory of a king honours matters. 
\q1
\v 3 Heaven is high, and earth is deep, 
\q2 and a king's heart is unsearchable. 
\q1
\v 4 Refine impure silver, 
\q2 and it will be made entirely pure.
\q1
\v 5 Kill the ungodly from before the king, 
\q2 and his throne will prosper in righteousness. 
\q1
\v 6 Don't be boastful in the presence of the king, 
\q2 and don't stand in the places of rulers; 
\q1
\v 7 for \add it is\add* better that it be said to you, Come up to me, 
\q2 than that you be humbled in the presence of the ruler. 
\q1 Speak of that which your eyes have seen. 
\q1
\v 8 Don't rush into a quarrel, 
\q2 lest you repent at last. 
\q1
\v 9 Whenever your friend reproaches you, 
\q2 draw back; don't despise \add him\add*, 
\q1
\v 10 lest your friend continue to reproach you, 
\q2 and your quarrel and enmity not depart, 
\q2 but be to you like death. 
\q1
\v 10a Favour and friendship set \add a man\add* free, 
\q2 keep them for yourself, lest you be brought into reproach; 
\q2 but take heed to your ways peaceably. 
\q1
\v 11 \add Like\add* a golden apple in a necklace of sardius, 
\q2 so is the speaking of a word. 
\q1
\v 12 In an earring of gold a precious sardius is also set; 
\q2 \add so is\add* a wise word to an obedient ear. 
\q1
\v 13 As a fall of snow in the time of harvest is refreshing in the heat, 
\q2 so is a faithful messenger to those who send him; 
\q2 for he benefits the souls of those who employ him. 
\q1
\v 14 As winds and clouds and rains are plainly seen, 
\q2 so are those who boast of a false gift. 
\q1
\v 15 By long-suffering comes prosperity to kings, 
\q2 and a soft tongue breaks the bones. 
\q1
\v 16 Having found honey, eat \add only\add* what is enough, 
\q2 lest you be filled, and vomit it up. 
\q1
\v 17 Enter sparingly in your friend's house, 
\q2 lest he be fed up with your company, and hate you. 
\q1
\v 18 \add As\add* a club, and a sword, and a pointed arrow, 
\q2 so also is a man who bears false witness against his friend. 
\q1
\v 19 The tooth of an evil man, 
\q2 and the foot of the transgressor 
\q2 will perish in an evil day. 
\q1
\v 20 As vinegar is harmful for a wound, 
\q2 so trouble befalling the body afflicts the heart. 
\q1
\v 20a As a moth in a garment, 
\q2 and a worm in wood, 
\q2 so a man's sorrow hurts the heart. 
\q1
\v 21 If your enemy is hungry, feed him; 
\q2 if he is thirsty, give him a drink; 
\q1
\v 22 for so doing you will heap coals of fire upon his head, 
\q2 and the Lord will reward you \add with\add* good. 
\q1
\v 23 The north wind raises clouds; 
\q2 but an impudent face stirs up the tongue. 
\q1
\v 24 \add It is\add* better to dwell on a corner of the roof, 
\q2 than with a quarrelsome woman in an open house. 
\q1
\v 25 As cold water is welcome to a thirsting soul, 
\q2 so is a good message from a land far off. 
\q1
\v 26 As if one should stop up a spring,
\q2 and spoil the flow of water, 
\q2 so \add is it\add* unseemly for a righteous man to fall before an ungodly man. 
\q1
\v 27 \add It is\add* not good to eat much honey; 
\q2 but it is right to honour noble words. 
\q1
\v 28 As a city whose walls are broken down, 
\q2 and which is unfortified, 
\q2 so is a man who does anything without counsel. 
\c 26 
\q1
\v 1 As dew in harvest, and as rain in summer, 
\q2 so honour is not for a fool. 
\q1
\v 2 As birds and sparrows fly, 
\q2 so a curse will not come upon anyone without a cause. 
\q1
\v 3 As a whip for a horse, and a goad for an ass, 
\q2 so \add is\add* a rod for a lawless nation. 
\q1
\v 4 Don't answer a fool according to his folly, 
\q2 lest you become like him. 
\q1
\v 5 Yet answer a fool according to his folly, 
\q2 lest he seem wise in his own conceit. 
\q1
\v 6 He who sends a message by a foolish messenger 
\q2 drinks reproach from his own feet. 
\q1
\v 7 Take away the motion of the legs, 
\q2 and a proverb from the mouth of fools. 
\q1
\v 8 He who releases a stone in a sling, 
\q2 is like one who gives glory to a fool. 
\q1
\v 9 Thorns grow in the hand of a drunkard, 
\q2 and servitude in the hand of fools. 
\q1
\v 10 All the flesh of fools endures much hardship; 
\q2 for their frenzy is broken. 
\q1
\v 11 As a dog returns to his own vomit and becomes hateful, 
\q2 so is a fool who returns in his wickedness to his own sin. 
\q1
\v 11a There is a shame that brings sin: 
\q2 and there is a shame \add that is\add* glory and grace.
\q1
\v 12 I have seen a man who seemed \f + \fr 26:12 \fqa Gr. \ft by.\f*to himself to be wise; 
\q2 but a fool has more hope than he. 
\q1
\v 13 A sluggard when sent on a journey says, \add There is\add* a lion in the ways. 
\q1
\v 14 As a door turns on the hinge, 
\q2 so does a sluggard on his bed. 
\q1
\v 15 A sluggard having hid his hand in his bosom, 
\q2 will not be able to bring it up to his mouth. 
\q1
\v 16 A sluggard seems wiser to himself 
\q2 than one who brings back a message well. 
\q1
\v 17 As he who lays hold of a dog's tail, 
\q2 so is he who makes himself the champion of another's cause. 
\q1
\v 18 As those \f + \fr 26:18 \fqa Or, \ft healing. The Greek is obscure.\f*under treatment put forth words to men, 
\q2 so the one who first answers the matter will be overthrown; 
\q1
\v 19 and so are all who lay wait for their own friends, 
\q2 and when they are uncovered, say, I was only joking. 
\q1
\v 20 With much wood fire increases; 
\q2 but where there is no divisive man, strife ceases. 
\q1
\v 21 A hearth for coals, and wood for fire; 
\q2 and a quarrelsome man for stirring up strife.
\q1
\v 22 The words of tricksters are soft; 
\q2 but they strike \add even\add* to the inmost parts of the bowels. 
\q1
\v 23 Silver dishonestly given is to be considered as a potsherd: 
\q2 smooth lips cover a grievous heart. 
\q1
\v 24 A weeping enemy promises all things with his lips, 
\q2 but in his heart he contrives deceit. 
\q1
\v 25 Though \add your\add* enemy entreat you with a loud voice, don't consent: 
\q2 for there are seven abominations in his heart. 
\q1
\v 26 He who hides enmity frames deceit: 
\q2 but being easily discerned, exposes his own sins in the public assemblies. 
\q1
\v 27 He who digs a pit for his neighbour will fall into it: 
\q2 and he who rolls a stone, rolls it upon himself. 
\q1
\v 28 A lying tongue hates the truth; 
\q2 and an unguarded mouth brings disorder. 
\c 27 
\q1
\v 1 Don't boast of tomorrow; 
\q2 for you don't know what the next day will bring forth. 
\q1
\v 2 Let your neighbour, and not your own mouth, praise you; 
\q2 a stranger, and not your own lips. 
\q1
\v 3 A stone is heavy, and sand cumbersome; 
\q2 but a fool's wrath is heavier than both. 
\q1
\v 4 Wrath is merciless, and anger sharp: 
\q2 but who can stand before jealousy?
\q1
\v 5 Open reproofs are better than secret love. 
\q1
\v 6 The wounds of a friend are more to be trusted 
\q2 than the spontaneous kisses of an enemy. 
\q1
\v 7 A full soul scorns honeycombs; 
\q2 but to a hungry soul even bitter things appear sweet. 
\q1
\v 8 As when a bird flies down from its own nest, 
\q2 so a man is enslaved when he is estranged from his own place. 
\q1
\v 9 The heart delights in ointments and wines and perfumes: 
\q2 but the soul is broken by calamities. 
\q1
\v 10 Don't forsake your own friend or your father's friend; 
\q2 and when you are in distress don't go into your brother's house: 
\q2 better is a friend nearby than a brother living far off. 
\q1
\v 11 Son, be wise, that my heart may rejoice; 
\q2 and remove you from yourself reproachful words. 
\q1
\v 12 A prudent man, when evils are approaching, hides himself; 
\q2 but fools pass on, and will be punished. 
\q1
\v 13 Take away his garment, for he has passed by; 
\q2 he is insolent who lays waste another's goods. 
\q1
\v 14 Whoever will bless a friend in the morning with a loud voice, 
\q2 will seem to be no different from one who curses. 
\q1
\v 15 On a stormy day drops \add of rain\add* drive a man out of his house; 
\q2 so also does a quarrelsome woman \add drive a man\add* out of his own house. 
\q1
\v 16 The north wind is sharp, 
\q2 but it is called by name Favourable. 
\q1
\v 17 Iron sharpens iron; 
\q2 and a man sharpens his friend's countenance. 
\q1
\v 18 He who plants a fig-tree will eat its fruits: 
\q2 so he who guards his master will be honoured. 
\q1
\v 19 As faces are not like \add other\add* faces, 
\q2 so neither are the hearts of men. 
\q1
\v 20 Hades and destruction are not filled; 
\q2 so also are the eyes of men insatiable. 
\q1
\v 20a He who fixes his gaze is an abomination to the Lord; 
\q2 and the uninstructed do not restrain their tongue.
\q1
\v 21 Fire is the trial for silver and gold; 
\q2 and a man is tried by the mouth of those who praise him. 
\q1
\v 21a The heart of the transgressor seeks evil; 
\q2 but an upright heart seeks knowledge. 
\q1
\v 22 Though you scourge a fool, disgracing him among the council, 
\q2 you will certainly not remove his folly from him. 
\q1
\v 23 Thoroughly know the condition of your flock, 
\q2 and pay attention to your herds. 
\q1
\v 24 For a man \add has\add* not strength and power forever; 
\q2 neither does he transmit it from generation to generation. 
\q1
\v 25 Take care of the plants in the field, 
\q2 and you will cut grass, and gather the mountain hay; 
\q1
\v 26 that you may have \add wool of\add* sheep for clothing: 
\q2 value the field, that you may have lambs. 
\q1
\v 27 \add My\add* son, you have from me words very useful for your life, 
\q2 and for the life of your servants. 
\c 28 
\p
\q1
\v 1 The ungodly \add man\add* flees when no one pursues: 
\q2 but the righteous is confident like a lion. 
\q1
\v 2 Because of the sins of ungodly men judgements arise; 
\q2 but a wise man will \f + \fr 28:2 \fqa Gr. \ft quench.\f*quell them. 
\q1
\v 3 A strong man oppresses the poor by ungodly deeds. 
\q2 As an impetuous and unprofitable rain, 
\q1
\v 4 so those who forsake the law praise ungodliness; 
\q2 but those who love the law fortify themselves with a wall. 
\q1
\v 5 Evil men will not understand judgment: 
\q2 but those who seek the Lord will understand \f + \fr 28:5 \fqa Gr. \ft in everything.\f*everything. 
\q1
\v 6 A poor man walking in truth is better than a rich liar. 
\q1
\v 7 A wise son keeps the law: 
\q2 but he who keeps company with debauchery dishonours his father. 
\q1
\v 8 He who increases his wealth by interest and \add unjust\add* gains, 
\q2 gathers it for him who pities the poor. 
\q1
\v 9 He who turns away his ear from hearing the law, 
\q2 even he has \f + \fr 28:9 \fqa Or, \ft abhorred his prayer.\f*made his prayer abominable. 
\q1
\v 10 He who causes upright men to err in an evil way, 
\q2 himself will fall into destruction: 
\q2 transgressors also will pass by prosperity, 
\q2 but will not enter into it. 
\q1
\v 11 A rich man is wise in his own conceit; 
\q2 but an intelligent poor man will see through him. 
\q1
\v 12 Because of the help of righteous men great glory arises: 
\q2 but in the places of the ungodly men are caught. 
\q1
\v 13 He who covers his own ungodliness will not prosper: 
\q2 but he who confesses his faults will be loved. 
\q1
\v 14 Blessed is the man who religiously fears \f + \fr 28:14 \fqa Gr. \ft all things.\f*always: 
\q2 but the hard of heart will fall into troubles. 
\q1
\v 15 A hungry lion and a thirsty wolf \add is he\add*, 
\q2 who, being poor, rules over a poor nation. 
\q1
\v 16 A king in need of revenues is a great oppressor: 
\q2 but he who hates injustice will live a long time. 
\q1
\v 17 He who becomes surety for a man charged with murder will be an exile, 
\q2 and not in safety. 
\q1
\v 17a Chasten your son, and he will love you, and give honour to your soul. 
\q2 Do not obey a sinful nation. 
\q1
\v 18 He who walks justly is assisted: 
\q2 but he who walks in crooked ways will be entangled. 
\q1
\v 19 He who tills his own land will be satisfied with \f + \fr 28:19 \fqa Gr. \ft plural.\f*bread: 
\q2 but he who follows idleness will be filled with poverty. 
\q1
\v 20 A faithful man will be much blessed: 
\q2 but the wicked will not be unpunished. 
\q1
\v 21 He who shows partiality is not good: 
\q2 such a one will sell a man for a morsel of bread. 
\q1
\v 22 An envious man makes haste to be rich, 
\q2 and doesn't know that the merciful man will have the mastery over him. 
\q1
\v 23 He who reproves a man's ways will have more favour 
\q2 than he who flatters with the tongue. 
\q1
\v 24 \x + \xo 28:24 \xt Mark 7. 11.\x*He who casts off father or mother, and thinks he doesn't sin; 
\q2 he is partaker with an ungodly man. 
\q1
\v 25 An greedy man judges rashly: 
\q2 but he who trusts in the Lord will act carefully. 
\q1
\v 26 He who trusts to a reckless heart, such an one is a fool: 
\q2 but he who walks in wisdom will be safe. 
\q1
\v 27 He who gives to the poor will not be in want: 
\q2 but he who turns away his eye \add from him\add* will be in great distress. 
\q1
\v 28 In the places of ungodly \add men\add* the righteous \f + \fr 28:28 \fqa Gr. \ft groan.\f*mourn: 
\q2 but in their destruction the righteous will be multiplied. 
\c 29 
\q1
\v 1 A reprover is better than a stiff-necked man: 
\q2 for when he suddenly burns \add with anger\add*, there will be no remedy. 
\q1
\v 2 When the righteous are praised, the people will rejoice: 
\q2 but when the ungodly rule, men mourn. 
\q1
\v 3 When a man loves wisdom, his father rejoices: 
\q2 but he who keeps harlots will waste wealth. 
\q1
\v 4 A righteous king establishes a country: 
\q2 but a transgressor destroys \add it\add*. 
\q1
\v 5 He who prepares a net in the way of his own friend, 
\q2 entangles his own feet in it. 
\q1
\v 6 A great snare \add is spread\add* for a sinner: 
\q2 but the righteous will be in joy and gladness. 
\q1
\v 7 A righteous man knows how to judge for the poor: 
\q2 but the ungodly doesn't understand knowledge; 
\q2 nor does he have understanding for the poor. 
\q1
\v 8 Corrupt men burn down a city: 
\q2 but wise men turn away wrath. 
\q1
\v 9 A wise man will judge nations: 
\q2 but a worthless man, when angry, is laughed at and shows no fear. 
\q1
\v 10 Bloody men hate a holy \add person\add*, 
\q2 but the upright will seek his soul. 
\q1
\v 11 A fool utters all his mind: 
\q2 but the wise reserves his in part. 
\q1
\v 12 When a king hearkens to unjust language, 
\q2 all his subjects are transgressors. 
\q1
\v 13 When the creditor and debtor meet together, 
\q2 the Lord oversees them both. 
\q1
\v 14 When a king judges the poor in truth, 
\q2 his throne will be established as a witness. 
\q1
\v 15 Stripes and reproofs give wisdom: 
\q2 but an erring child disgraces his parents. 
\q1
\v 16 When the ungodly abound, sins abound: 
\q2 but when they fall, the righteous become afraid. 
\q1
\v 17 Chasten your son, and he will give you rest; 
\q2 and he will give honour to your soul. 
\q1
\v 18 There will be no interpreter to a sinful nation: 
\q2 but he who observes the law is \f + \fr 29:18 \fqa Or, \ft 'most blessed.'\f*blessed. 
\q1
\v 19 A stubborn servant will not be reproved by words: 
\q2 for even if he understands, still he will not obey. 
\q1
\v 20 If you see a man hasty in \add his\add* words, 
\q2 know that a fool has more hope than he. 
\q1
\v 21 He who pampers a servant from childhood, 
\q2 in the end will grieve over himself. 
\q1
\v 22 A furious man stirs up strife, 
\q2 and a quick-tempered man brings forth sin. 
\q1
\v 23 Pride brings a man low, 
\q2 but the Lord upholds the humble-minded with honour. 
\q1
\v 24 He who shares with a thief, hates his own soul: 
\q2 and if, when an oath is uttered, he hears it and doesn't report it,
\q1
\v 25 \add those\add* fearing and reverencing men \add unreasonably\add* have been overthrown, 
\q2 but he who trusts in the Lord will rejoice. 
\q1 Ungodliness causes a man to stumble: 
\q2 but he who trusts in \f + \fr 29:25 \ft Possibly, 'in the Lord.' See \xt 2 Pet. 2. 1.\f*his master will be safe. 
\q1
\v 26 Many court the favour of rulers; 
\q2 but justice comes to a man from the Lord. 
\q1
\v 27 A righteous man is an abomination to an unrighteous man, 
\q2 and an upright way is an abomination to the lawless. 
\c 30 
\v 1 \f + \fr 30:1 \ft See chapter 24 for the content of chapter 30.\f* 
\c 31 
\p
\q1
\v 10 Who will find a capable woman? 
\q2 For one like her is more valuable than precious stones. 
\q1
\v 11 The heart of her husband trusts in her: 
\q2 one like her will not lack noble spoils. 
\q1
\v 12 For she employs all her living for her husband's good. 
\q1
\v 13 Gathering wool and flax, 
\q2 she works them skilfully with her hands. 
\q1
\v 14 She is like a ship trading from a distance: 
\q2 so she procures her livelihood. 
\q1
\v 15 She rises by night, and gives food to her household, 
\q2 and \add appointed\add* tasks to her maidens. 
\q1
\v 16 She views a farm, and buys it: 
\q2 and with the fruit of her hands she plants an estate. 
\q1
\v 17 She strongly girds her loins, 
\q2 and strengthens her arms for work. 
\q1
\v 18 She finds by experience that working is good; 
\q2 and her candle doesn't go out all night. 
\q1
\v 19 She stretches out her arms to useful work, 
\q2 and applies her hands to the spindle. 
\q1
\v 20 She opens her hands to the needy, 
\q2 and extends the fruit of her labour to the poor. 
\p
\q1
\v 21 Her husband isn't anxious about those at home when he is delayed somewhere: 
\q2 for all \f + \fr 31:21 \fqa Gr. \ft those with her.\f*her household are clothed. 
\q1
\v 22 She makes double garments for her husband, 
\q2 and garments for herself of fine linen and scarlet. 
\q1
\v 23 Her husband becomes distinguished at the gates, 
\q2 when he sits in council with the old inhabitants of the land. 
\q1
\v 24 She made fine linens and sold them, and girdles to the Canaanites.
\q2 She opens her mouth with care and propriety, and controls her tongue. 
\q1
\v 25 She puts on strength and dignity; 
\q2 and rejoices in the last days. 
\q1
\v 26 The ways of her household are careful, 
\q2 and she doesn't eat the bread of idleness. 
\q1
\v 27 But she opens her mouth wisely, 
\q2 and according to law. 
\q1
\v 28 And \add her\add* kindness has raised up her children,
\q2 and they have grown rich, 
\q2 and her husband has praised her. 
\q1
\v 29 Many daughters have obtained wealth, many have worked valiantly; 
\q2 but you have exceeded, you have surpassed all. 
\q1
\v 30 Charms are false, and woman's beauty is vain: 
\q2 for it is a wise woman that is blessed, 
\q2 and let her praise the fear of the Lord. 
\q1
\v 31 Give her of the fruit of her hands; 
\q2 and let her husband be praised in the gates. 
